 "drivingQuestion": "From where is it derived that private altars were permitted at Nov and Gibeon?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Answer begins with the baraita, continued in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "zevachim.119a.3",
   "daf": "119a",
   "sentenceId": 2,
   "he": "בָּאוּ לְנוֹב וְגִבְעוֹן [וְכוּ׳]. מְנָא הָנֵי מִילֵּי? דְּתָנוּ רַבָּנַן: ״כִּי לֹא בָּאתֶם עַד עָתָּה אֶל הַמְּנוּחָה וְאֶל הַנַּחֲלָה״;",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that when Shiloh was destroyed and they «arrived at Nov and Gibeon,» private altars were permitted and offerings of lesser sanctity could be eaten in any city in Eretz Yisrael. The Gemara asks: «From where are these matters» derived? they are derived «as the Sages taught:» The Jewish people were told that when they enter Eretz Yisrael they would be permitted to sacrifice on private altars, «“for you have not as yet come to the rest and to the inheritance”» (Deuteronomy 12:9), during which time those altars would be prohibited."
  }
 ],
